@page "/components/data-grid"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.DataGrid

<DocsHeader Title="Data grid" Eyebrow="Data">
    <Lead>A table for business data: sorting, filters, paging, selection, virtualization and server-side data.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    <code>StilDataGrid</code> renders a <code>stil-table</code>. Each <code>StilDataGridColumn</code> binds to a property,
    and the same expression shows, sorts and filters the column, also in a database query. For a simple list of rows,
    the lighter <a href="components/tables">table</a> is enough.
</p>

<h2 id="sorting">Sorting and paging</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(SortingAndPaging)">
    <p>
        Clicking a header sorts ascending, then descending, then not at all; the header has <code>aria-sort</code>.
        <code>InitialSort</code> sorts when the grid first shows. <code>Title</code> defaults to the property name,
        <code>Format</code> formats the value in the current culture, and <code>Width</code> fixes a column's width.
        <code>PageSize</code> is 10 by default (0 shows every row), and <code>PageSizeOptions</code> lets the user choose.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="filtering">Filters and templates</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Filtering)">
    <p>
        <code>Filterable</code> adds a filter row. Text columns get a case-insensitive "contains" filter that waits for
        typing to pause; enum and bool columns get a choice; <code>Filterable="false"</code> leaves a column out. A
        <code>Template</code> changes how a cell looks without changing what it sorts and filters on.
        <code>TrueText</code> and <code>FalseText</code> name the values of a bool column.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="selection">Selection and the toolbar</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Selection)">
    <p>
        With <code>SelectionMode="Multiple"</code> a checkbox column appears, and the header checkbox selects every row
        on the page. Bind <code>@@bind-SelectedItems</code>. Set <code>ItemKey</code> when the rows are new objects on
        every load, so the selection is kept. <code>Toolbar</code> holds actions above the grid. A
        <code>StilDataGridTemplateColumn</code> holds anything, such as buttons; it cannot be sorted or filtered.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="single">Single selection and row styles</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(RowClicks)">
    <p>
        <code>SelectionMode="Single"</code> selects a row on a click, or with Enter when the row has focus; bind
        <code>@@bind-SelectedItem</code>. Use <code>OnRowClick</code> to open a row instead. <code>RowClass</code> adds
        classes to a row; do not use a style alone to show a state, so this grid also says it in a column.
        <code>EmptyContent</code> replaces the "No items" row.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="totals">Totals and export</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(TotalsAndExport)">
    <p>
        <code>Aggregate</code> on a column (<code>Sum</code>, <code>Average</code>, <code>Count</code>, <code>Min</code> or
        <code>Max</code>) adds a footer row with the total of every filtered row, not only the page, in the column's
        <code>Format</code>. <code>AggregateValue</code> computes a total of your own, such as a weighted average.
        <code>ShowExport</code> adds a button that downloads the filtered and sorted rows of every page as CSV;
        <code>ExportCsvAsync()</code> returns the text. Numbers are written without thousands separators and the separator
        is the culture's, so Excel opens the file as it is. A template column is exported when it has <code>ExportValue</code>.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="column-layout">Column layout</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(ColumnLayout)">
    <p>
        <code>ResizableColumns</code> lets the user drag the edge of a header, or focus it and use the arrow keys
        (<code>MinWidth</code> and <code>MaxWidth</code> set the limits). <code>ReorderableColumns</code> lets them drag a
        header to another place, and <code>ShowColumnChooser</code> adds a Columns button that hides, shows and moves
        columns, also with the keyboard. <code>Frozen</code> keeps a column at the start or end while the grid scrolls
        sideways. A column can opt out with <code>Resizable</code>, <code>Reorderable</code> and <code>Hideable</code>.
        <code>@@bind-Layout</code> gives the layout (order, widths, hidden columns and sort) as a
        <code>StilDataGridLayout</code>, which the app can save, for example per user, and give back.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="grouping">Grouping</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Grouping)">
    <p>
        <code>@@bind-GroupBy</code> groups the rows by one or more columns (their Keys, outermost first), and
        <code>ShowGroupChooser</code> adds a Group by button that lets the user pick them. Each group starts with a header
        row that shows its value, its number of rows and the totals of the columns with an <code>Aggregate</code>. The
        header is a button: click it, or press Enter, Space or the left and right arrow keys, to collapse and expand the
        group; the toolbar expands and collapses all of them. Paging counts rows: a group that continues on the next page
        shows its header again there, and the rows of a collapsed group leave the pages. Sorting by a grouped column
        orders its groups; other sorts order the rows within each group. <code>Groupable="false"</code> keeps a column
        out of the chooser.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="server-data">Server-side data</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(ServerData)">
    <p>
        With <code>ItemsProvider</code> the grid asks for one page at a time. The request has <code>StartIndex</code>,
        <code>Count</code>, <code>SortBy</code>, <code>SortDirection</code>, <code>Filters</code> and a
        <code>CancellationToken</code> that is cancelled when the grid asks again. For an <code>IQueryable</code> such as
        an EF Core <code>DbSet</code>, <code>ApplyFiltersAndSorting</code> and <code>ApplyPaging</code> build the query
        from the columns. <code>ComputeAggregates</code> computes the totals in the same way, so EF Core runs them in the
        database; return them in the result. When the rows are grouped, <code>GroupBy</code> lists the grouped columns,
        <code>ApplyFiltersAndSorting</code> sorts by them first and <code>ComputeGroups</code> returns the groups with
        their counts and totals (a few queries per group). An export pages through the provider. Call <code>RefreshAsync()</code>
        through <code>@@ref</code> to reload.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="virtualization">Virtualization</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Virtualized)">
    <p>
        <code>Virtualize</code> with a <code>Height</code> renders only the visible rows of a long list instead of pages.
        The header stays visible while you scroll. Every row should have the same height (<code>ItemSize</code>, 41 px by
        default).
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="render-modes">Render modes</h2>
<p>
    In static server rendering the grid shows its first page, sorted by <code>InitialSort</code>. Sorting, filters,
    paging and selection need an interactive render mode.
</p>

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilDataGrid<>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilDataGridColumn<,>)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilDataGridTemplateColumn<>)" />
